# config/generation.toml declares line_endings = "lf" as the single
# authoritative policy for every committed generated schema: `generate`
# always writes LF, so a byte-for-byte drift check only ever passes if
# the committed bytes stay LF too. Without this file, a Windows Git
# client's default core.autocrlf behavior converts the committed LF
# bytes to CRLF on checkout, and `generate --check` then reports drift
# against content that was never actually regenerated -- purely a
# checkout-time conversion, not a real difference (observed live in
# cross-platform-mage.yml run 30075727901 on windows-latest: every
# committed schema under schemas/ reported as drifted).
schemas/** text eol=lf

# Same issue, different files: tests/golden/*.json are byte-for-byte
# comparison fixtures for deterministic JSON encoders (EncodeManifest
# and others). Every one is committed LF-only; without this rule a
# Windows checkout's core.autocrlf conversion makes every such test fail
# even after the encoder's own platform-key coupling is fixed (observed
# live: cross-platform-mage.yml run 30110425773 -- tests/golden/
# foundation-manifest.json failed EncodeManifest's golden-fixture test
# on windows-latest with visually identical "got"/"want" JSON bodies,
# because the mismatch was in trailing line-ending bytes, not content).
tests/golden/** text eol=lf

# Same issue again: CheckMigration (internal/trace/catalog/migrate.go)
# compares the committed .planning/linear-map.json byte-for-byte against
# a freshly re-derived canonical schema-2 encoding, which strictjson
# always emits LF-only -- so without this rule a Windows checkout's
# core.autocrlf conversion makes the committed file fail its own drift
# check even though nothing about the map actually drifted (observed
# live: cross-platform-mage.yml run 30112915317 on windows-latest).
.planning/linear-map.json text eol=lf
